# Story writing — hard rules

These bind whenever drafting or editing chapters, dialogue, or in-world narration. Lore / outline / PROGRESS are **writer-only** unless a character has earned the fact on-page.

## No writer-only leaks

Never put any of the following into dialogue, narration, or a character's thoughts unless that camera has already learned it in-story (or it is purely sensory/public):

- Forks, stage maps, "soft fork / hard fork," corruption stages, volume titles, chapter plans
- Outline labels (A-plot, B-plot, planted twist, payoff, bible unpaid, camera moment as jargon)
- Magic system jargon the boys haven't been taught (King's Word name, Literal→Intent, Backwash as a named rule, Rewrite, One Throne, etc.) — prologue restraint especially
- Meta address to the reader about craft ("I want you to understand," filing-as-thesis dumps that exist to teach the audience the outline)
- Continuity tracker language, archive notes, "in the rewrite," "for later volumes"

If it lives in `lore/`, `PROGRESS.md`, or `README.md` as **writer scaffolding**, it does not speak through a character's mouth.

## Knowledge hygiene

- **People only talk about what they actually know.** No psychic exposition. No guessing that lands as fact.
- **New characters do not bleed old characters' secrets.** A stranger cannot recite the roof, the birthmark, Mira's pitch, or Remnant doctrine unless they were there, were briefed, or have a shown channel (file, rumor with uncertainty, radio).
- **POV camera is not omniscient.** Guest or multi-POV cuts only know their own sensory present + their own past. No borrowing another POV's private ledger.
- **Rumors stay labeled as rumors.** Wrong or partial information is allowed; confident false certainty from someone who couldn't know is not.
- **FDC / Remnant / Order** may know institutional facts; they still don't know a boy's private guilt or an off-page conversation they didn't hear.

## Progression and repetition

- **Smooth progression.** Each scene advances a want, a cost, or a relationship. No stalling loops that restate the same beat with new adjectives.
- **No repetitiveness.** Don't re-explain the roof, the Word tell, the birthmark, or a vow every chapter. One clear hit; later callbacks are short and earned.
- **No motif spam.** Cap ledger/filed/litany language; vary opens and closes; don't end consecutive chapters on sleep/bed/"tomorrow."

## HARD — No thin recap stacks / no pre-break buttons

**Never** close a beat, scene, or chapter by restating what just happened in short summary lines. That is outline voice, not story.

**Especially before a scene break (`---`):** do not park a neat little button, moral, or status line that "wraps" the section. If the exchange is done, either cut straight to `---` on the last real line of talk/action, or keep moving inside the scene. Do not add a narrator stamp like "She does not make a speech about it" / "I do not tell him why" / "He drinks. Not much. Enough."

If the reader already saw it, do not file it again. Move to the next action, line of dialogue, or decision.

- Bad: "The tool shops stay shut. Nobody looks like Kaito. Nobody looks like Mira. The water tastes like warm plastic."
- Bad: "He drinks. She does not make a speech about it." ← then `---`
- Bad: "I do not tell him why I am looking at him like that." ← then `---`
- Bad: "No stamp left. No Mira. No Kaito." / "One day behind. Still walking."
- Good: End the section on spoken dialogue or a concrete next move ("Drink anyway." / He drinks. / next section starts).
- Good: After the rumor, go straight to the next move — ask again, start walking — without a bullet summary.

Same ban on "ledger, plain:" / status-board narration. Status lives in `PROGRESS.md`, not in the prose.

## HARD — No inventory talk

**Never** write telegram / checklist speech — stacked stubs, noun-chains, or fragment lists that "sound cool" instead of how people talk or think.

This ban applies to **dialogue and narration**. Blunt characters still use full sentences. Short can be short; it cannot be a bullet list with periods.

- Bad: "Further in. Girl with him. They washed my cups. They didn't bring soldiers. That's all I sell."
- Bad: "Haiga dust. The gray. Doesn't wash out." / "School roof. Midnight. Whole city. Cake." / "Midnight. All three."
- Good: "They went further inland. The girl was with him. They washed my cups and they didn't bring soldiers — that's all I'm telling you."
- Good: "He had Haiga dust on his shoes — that gray from the east. You can't wash it out."
- Allowed clips only when panic, orders, or radio procedure earn them (*On the ground.* / *Last warning.*) — not as default voice for shopkeepers, friends, or POV narration.

## Dialogue — real talk, not poetry or telegrams

Characters talk to each other in **plain, normal sentences**. No epigram dialogue. No thesis lines. No stylized farewell wisdom. No inventory talk (see above).

- People say what they mean for the moment: facts, asks, refusals, fear, anger.
- Do **not** write lines that sound like chapter titles, morals, or literary punch lines.
- Bad: "I'm done being useful to people with lists." / "Pick the work that still has feet." / "Should is a religion for people with spare rooms."
- Good: "I can't help you with this." / "Go after him, or stay. Pick one." / "Should doesn't matter. Choose."
- Voice still differs (Sugi blunt, Mira mild, Sato sharp) — difference is diction and temper, not poetry or stub-chains.

### HARD — Conversations must engage

Do **not** write plot-handoff dialogue: one short line each, no pushback, no confusion, no leftover human noise. That reads like skipping, not talking.

- People interrupt, ask the wrong follow-up, repeat themselves, refuse part of the ask, get irritated, bargain, or answer sideways.
- Important info can still land — but through a real exchange, not a single clean dump.
- Bad: `"Which way."` / `"Ishibashi."` / `"Thank you."` / `"Don't thank me."`
- Good: Haruki has to explain who he means; Orii makes him work for it; he almost over-explains; she cuts him off; he still gets the road.
- Blunt characters stay blunt — blunt is not the same as one-sentence teleport.

## Narration — story prose, not poetry / haiku

**North star:** `assets/example-chapter-1`. That voice tells a story in full sentences: what the character is doing, why it matters, what they notice because it changes the next move. Write like that.

This is **not** a lyric novella. Do not turn every paragraph into mood, metaphor, or punch-line fragments.

- Prefer: cause → action → result. Explain enough that a reader can follow without decoding.
- Prefer: longer clear beats over stacked one-line images.
- Prefer: "I ask at the depot because that is where buses leave" over "the town forgets salt / heat on doorframes / afternoon gone flat and white."
- Avoid: personified roads/weather, ornamental closes, thesis feelings every other line, haiku stubs ("I am." / "Still walking." / "One day behind.") used as style.
- Avoid: mosaic travel montages that skip the story for atmosphere.
- Bad (haiku drift): "The road forgets the harbor smell. Scrapyard fence. Canal ditch. Still walking."
- Bad (poetry clause): "Part of me still thinks Kaito leaves heat on doorframes — scrapes on a lintel, a laugh stuck in an alley…"
- Good (story): "I already used Sato's stamp at the first checkpoint, so this one I have to talk through. The soldier checks my shoes, then my face. I tell him I am looking for family west of the ridge. He lets me past."
- Good (example texture): put detail on the task — lockpicks, room layout, why the plan changes — not on pretty weather.
- Feeling is allowed when it is short and attached to a choice. Not a meditation replacing the scene.
- Cut on action, speech, or the next concrete step — never on a literary button.

## When unsure

Prefer silence, partial understanding, or wrong conclusions over a convenient info dump. Writer truth can wait; character truth cannot be faked.
